= An anyon fringe as a one-sided test for Dedekind GRH

Let $K$ be the splitting field of $X^3-X-1$ and let $E=QQ(sqrt(-23))$ be its quadratic subfield. There is a nonnegative prime-power weight computed by winding an electric probe in $D(S_3)$. A dyadic prime sum made from that weight detects every critical-strip zero of $zeta_K$. In particular, a square-root upper bound on arbitrarily large multiplicative windows is equivalent, with the usual arbitrarily small exponent loss, to GRH for this one field.

== Reading a Frobenius coefficient

The standard two-dimensional representation $V$ of $S_3$ has character $chi=(2,0,-1)$ on the identity, transpositions, and three-cycles. Denote its electric probe by $C$. For a target of flux $g$, independently of the charge attached to that flux, the normalized fringe of $m$ coherent windings is $W_m=chi(g^m)/2$. The balancing law, or the electric action of $g$ on $V$, gives this formula. [#s3]

At each unramified prime substitute the Frobenius conjugacy class $g_p$ for the flux. Set $b(p^m)=4 abs(W_m)^2$. The character identity
$ abs(chi)^2 = chi_(V ⊗ V^*) = 1 + epsilon + chi $
uses the sign character $epsilon$. Its values are $4,0,1$. Thus the squared fringe is both nonnegative at each good prime power and a sum of irreducible characters with positive multiplicities. On this group it also counts the number of square roots of $g_p^m$, as direct enumeration shows. The prime supplies the flux label to this arithmetic model.

Writing $L(s,V)$ for the standard Artin $L$-function, define
$ H(s) = zeta(s) L(s,epsilon) L(s,V), quad
  zeta_K (s) = zeta(s) L(s,epsilon) L(s,V)^2. $
The second identity is the regular representation factorization. The standard factor is entire, coming from the weight-one cusp form $eta(z) eta(23z)$. Thus $H$ has a simple pole at one and the same critical-strip zero set as $zeta_K$, with possibly different multiplicities. Both identities include the ramified Euler factors. At $23$ the inertia group is generated by a transposition (the cubic discriminant is squarefree), and its normalizer is itself. Residue Frobenius acts trivially on the two-dimensional inertia invariants of $V ⊗ V^*$. Consequently $b(23^m)=2$ for every $m>=1$. [#serre]

In particular the degree-four function $H$ has the logarithmic derivative
$ frac(-H' (s), H(s))=sum_(p "prime") sum_(m>=1) b(p^m) log(p) p^(-m s) quad (upright("Re") s>1), $
where the sum runs over primes and positive powers. The two factors of $L(s,V)$ in the degree-six regular representation have become one. For tests that seek the locations of zeros, one positive copy is enough.

#pagebreak()
= The input to the Landau window theorem

Write $Psi_H (t)=sum_(p^m<=t) b(p^m)log p$. Define the observable
$ B(x)=2x integral_x^(2x) (t-Psi_H (t))/t^2 dif t. $
It measures a deficit of weighted prime powers. A positive value means that the prime sum has fallen below its main term in this average. It uses primes and their powers only through $2x$.

*Criterion.* Fix $delta>0$, for instance $delta=1/2$. GRH for $zeta_K$ is equivalent to the following condition. For every $0<epsilon.alt<1/2$ there is a finite $C_(epsilon.alt)>0$ and there are arbitrarily large $Y$ such that
$ B(x)<=C_(epsilon.alt) x^(1/2+epsilon.alt) quad "for all" x in [Y,Y^(1+delta)]. $

*Proof of the window implication.* Use the same ramp kernel as the repository, with $R(x)=sum_(p^m<=x)b(p^m)log(p)(x/p^m-1)$. Interchanging the finite sum and integral gives $B(x)=2x log 2+2R(x)-R(2x)$. This dyadic difference cancels any term linear in $x$, including the unknown regular part of the logarithmic derivative at one. For $upright("Re") s>1$, the Mellin transform on $[1,infinity)$ is
$ Phi(s)= (2-2^s)/(s(s-1)) (frac(-H' (s), H(s)))+(2 log 2)/(s-1). $
Indeed the ramp transform is $frac(-H' slash H, s(s-1))$ and $R(t)=0$ for $1<=t<=2$. At $s=1$ the two terms have opposite residues. At a zero $rho$ of $H$ with multiplicity $m_rho$, the residue is
$ (m_rho (2-2^rho))/(rho(1-rho)). $
It is nonzero in the open critical strip and positive at every real zero there. This latter sign is needed for the repository’s one-sided theorem. The finite ramp makes $g(u)=B(e^u)$ continuous with the growth bound in #raw("Deduction.PoleData"). The Mellin identity gives its Laplace-transform field. Meromorphy of $H$, its isolated zeros, and nonvanishing on the line $upright("Re") s=1$ after treatment of the pole give the remaining analyticity requirements. At $s=1$ use the removable extension of $Phi$.

Apply #raw("Deduction.landau_window") with $theta=1/2+epsilon.alt$. On each window the majorant $C_(epsilon.alt) e^(theta u)$ meets #raw("WindowHypU") since its weighted integral on $[U,(1+delta)U]$ is $C_(epsilon.alt) delta U<=e^(eta U)$ eventually for each $eta>0$. Thus every zero has $upright("Re") rho<=1/2+epsilon.alt$. Taking all $epsilon.alt$ and using the Dedekind functional equation gives GRH.

*Converse.* The factorization gives $H^2=zeta_K zeta_E$ and $zeta_K/zeta_E=L(s,V)^2$. Under GRH for $zeta_K$, the zeros of $zeta_E$ in the strip are also on the line. Hence $Psi_H=(Psi_K+Psi_E)/2=t+O_K(sqrt(t) log^2 (2t))$ by the prime ideal theorem under GRH. Its integral gives the required upper bound for all sufficiently large $x$. [#pit]

