= The second probe and a finite certificate

== What the magnetic phase means arithmetically

The splitting field $K$ is the Hilbert class field of $E$. Indeed $K/E$ is cyclic of degree three and unramified, as the inertia computation at 23 shows. The three reduced forms of discriminant $-23$ give class number three. Choose a nontrivial class character $xi$ and put $omega=e^((2pi i)/3)$. For a rational prime split in $E$, choosing one of the prime ideals $frak(p)$ above it gives $xi(frak(p)) in {1,omega,overline(omega)}$. The conjugate ideal gives the inverse phase.

Encode that ideal class in the earlier protocol’s fixed three-cycle flux sector, with its centralizer character giving the twist $xi(frak(p))$. In order of twist, the sectors are $F,G,H$ (the last letter here labels an anyon). Winding a transposition-flux probe $D$ twice reads exactly this phase: $D$ fused with any of these three targets has outputs $D,E$ with twists $+1,-1$, so squaring the monodromy gives $xi(frak(p))^(-2)=xi(frak(p))$. This is an explicit choice of encoding. Switching the class character conjugates all the phases.

Inducing $xi$ from the quadratic field gives $V$. For split primes the descent to $QQ$ therefore gives
$ chi(g_p^m) = xi(frak(p))^m + overline(xi(frak(p)))^m. $
For an inert rational prime the Frobenius is a transposition, with traces zero in odd degree and two in even degree. For example, the two primes over $2$ give $omega,overline(omega)$ and the rational trace is $-1$. This distinguishes the information available at a prime ideal from the conjugacy-invariant coefficient needed in the rational Euler product.

== What the calculation certifies

The accompanying standard-library Python program computes the Frobenius order at each good prime using the discriminant character and the test $X^p=X$ modulo $(p,X^3-X-1)$. It independently checks every resulting $b(p^m)$ through $65 536$ against the logarithmic coefficients obtained from the eta product. For the magnetic readout it reduces both prime ideals above every split prime through $1000$ to their binary quadratic form classes. [#serre]

On the real interval $[1024,32768]$ it certifies
$ B(x)<=1.20 sqrt(x). $
The function $B$ is affine between the breakpoints $p^m$ and $p^m/2$. Subtracting $c sqrt(x)$ for $c>=0$ is convex on each such segment. Bounding its endpoints therefore covers every real $x$ in the interval. The check uses integer intervals for the logarithms from the atanh series, rounds all sums outwards, and squares the final comparisons in rational arithmetic.

The all-scales implication uses the abstract theorem in #raw("DedekindLFunctions/LandauTheorems/landauWindow.lean") at line 83. The Artin specialization and dyadic transform here are paper arguments. The certificate establishes one window. An application to GRH would have to produce windows with the stated bounds at unbounded scales; the existing abelian host estimates would require a further argument to supply them for this statistic.

#pagebreak()
= The estimate the abelian argument still needs

The field example isolates a particular prime-counting task. Put
$ h_x (t)=min(1, max(0, (2x)/t-1)). $
Write $S_E (x)$ for the sum of $h_x (p) log p$ over unramified rational primes split in the quadratic field $E$, and $S_0 (x)$ for the same sum over primes split completely in $K$. The latter are exactly the unramified primes represented by $a^2+a b+6b^2$, the principal norm form of discriminant $-23$. Count a represented prime once regardless of how many representations it has. [#serre]

The three character values give the identity on unramified primes
$ b(p) = bold(1)_(p " split in " E) + 3 bold(1)_(p " split completely in " K). $
For a split prime of $E$, the principal-class indicator is $(1+xi(frak(p))+overline(xi(frak(p))))/3$. This is precisely the Fourier projection that can be formed from the second winding. Its real value is independent of the choice of conjugate prime ideal.

Thus, writing $T(x)$ for the ramified prime and all higher prime powers, one obtains
$ B(x)=2x log 2 - S_E (x)-3S_0 (x)-T(x), quad
  0<=T(x) = O(sqrt(x) log^2 (2x)). $
Indeed the remaining coefficients are bounded by four. For each exponent $m>=2$ the primes have size at most $sqrt(2x)$, and there are at most $log_2 (2x)$ possible exponents. That elementary error is absorbed by $x^(1/2+epsilon.alt)$ for every fixed $epsilon.alt>0$.

*Conditional reduction to a single form.* Assume the classical GRH estimates for the two abelian factors $zeta(s)$ and $L(s,epsilon)$, equivalently GRH for $zeta_E$. Then $S_E (x)=x log 2 + O_E(sqrt(x) log^2 (2x))$. To see the normalization, each split rational prime contributes twice to the degree-one prime terms of $Psi_E$, and inert prime ideals have norm $p^2$. Apply the same integration as on page 2. After substitution, GRH for $zeta_K$ is equivalent to the following lower bound. For every $0<epsilon.alt<1/2$ there is a constant $A_(epsilon.alt)$ and there are arbitrarily large $Y$ on whose entire window
$ S_0 (x) >= (x log 2)/3 - A_(epsilon.alt) x^(1/2+epsilon.alt)
    quad (Y<=x<=Y^(3/2)). $

The program checked the principal norm form against all unramified primes through $65536$ and found 1072 completely split primes. Applying the same integer interval method to the 1575 knots of this ordinary-prime sum certifies $S_0 (x)>=(x log 2)/3 - 1.06 sqrt(x)$ for every real $x in [1024,32768]$.

This gives a concrete target for an extension of the atlas’s coset-weight method. The finite quotient is the order-three *ideal class group of an imaginary quadratic field*. Rational Dirichlet GRH supplies the quadratic background in the conditional reduction. A separate uniform estimate for the principal ideal class must supply the displayed lower bound. Sharpening a finite winding measurement or enumerating more bounded windows cannot establish that uniform estimate.
